[{"id":17218,"sender_id":36,"sender_full_name":"Harry Gindi","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8582ed155673829a9ea6642f5d1ca2ed?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17218\">#1</a> In Cruttwell-Shulman, there is a table in the introduction showing that a monoid (this terminology is not (yet?) standard) for the identity monad on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">V</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{V}-\\mathrm{mat}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7694em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\" style=\"margin-right:0.016em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mat</span></span></span></span></span> is a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\" style=\"margin-right:0.016em;\">V</span></span></span></span>-enriched category (can all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\" style=\"margin-right:0.016em;\">V</span></span></span></span>-enriched categories be realized this way?).  Thinking about enrichment this way, it seems like we should need the following to define the enrichment of a quasicategory in a monoidal quasicategory:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A notion of a monoidal quasicategory</li>\n<li>A quasicategorical analogue of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">V</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{V}-\\mathrm{Mat}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7694em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\" style=\"margin-right:0.016em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Mat</span></span></span></span></span></li>\n<li>A sufficiently weak notion of a monad.</li>\n<li>A sufficiently weak notion for a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span></span></span></span>-monoid for a monad <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span></span></span></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What are the technical barriers to using this to define the enrichment of a quasicategory?  Are there any obvious pitfalls that I’m missing?</p>","timestamp":1287298459,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17241,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17241\">#2</a> The only technical barriers that I’m aware of involve actually defining all of those things.  (-:  Although of course if you just want enriched (non-multi) categories, then the monad bit is unnecessary (since it would be the identity monad).  I’ve thought a bit off and on about “quasi- (virtual) double categories” but it never seemed worthwhile to do all the work pushing it through.</p>\n<p>PS. Yes, all V-enriched categories can be realized this way – it’s an equivalent <em>definition</em> of “V-enriched category.”</p>","timestamp":1287355268,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17242,"sender_id":36,"sender_full_name":"Harry Gindi","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8582ed155673829a9ea6642f5d1ca2ed?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17242\">#3</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I’ve thought a bit off and on about “quasi- (virtual) double categories” but it never seemed worthwhile to do all the work pushing it through.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Well, this would give a straightforward construction of so-called <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\infty,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>-categories by iterative enrichment, so isn’t it worthwhile?</p>","timestamp":1287361024,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17244,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17244\">#4</a> For a suitable definition of “straightforward,” and if you manage to play the right games with terminal coalgebras to boost yourself up from (∞,n) to (∞,∞), maybe.  My intuition is that it would end up being a good deal more complicated than most of the existing approaches to (∞,∞)-categories, but if you want to prove me wrong, more power to you.  (-:  If all you want to do is iteratively define (∞,n)-categroies starting from quasicategories, though, then I believe Joyal has suggested a way that might be simpler, using “reduced” internal categories in a quasicategory (kind of like complete Segal spaces, but done entirely in the quasicategorical world).</p>","timestamp":1287387147,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17245,"sender_id":36,"sender_full_name":"Harry Gindi","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8582ed155673829a9ea6642f5d1ca2ed?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17245\">#5</a> Do you have a reference?</p>","timestamp":1287387237,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17247,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17247\">#6</a> [1] A. Joyal.  Talk given at the Fields Institute conference on Higher Categories and their Applications, January 2007.</p>\n<p>Sorry....</p>","timestamp":1287391736,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17254,"sender_id":36,"sender_full_name":"Harry Gindi","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8582ed155673829a9ea6642f5d1ca2ed?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17254\">#7</a> Thanks!  What are you sorry for?</p>","timestamp":1287403875,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":17277,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Enriched.20quasicategories.20.28after.20reading.20the.20introduction.20of.E2.80.A6/near/17277\">#8</a> Not having a reference you can actually read.  (-:</p>","timestamp":1287442324,"subject":"Enriched quasicategories (after reading the introduction of…","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]}]